<ontology/use_soap_manufacture/use_soap_manufacture__0562__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 936" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n936/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Rosin used in Soap Manufacture: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 936"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Soap, made with tallow and soda, has a white colour, and therefore known by the name of white soap; but it is usual for soap-makers, in order to lower the price of the article, to mix a considerable portion of rosin with the tallow; this mixture forms the common yellow or turpentine soap."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .
